      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The impact of the hepatic branch of the vagus and Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RYGB) on the hypoglycemic effect and glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) in rats with type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) was investigated, and interactions were preliminarily analyzed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A total of 45 rats with T2DM were divided into four groups: sham operation (S, n = 10), sham operation with the hepatic branch of the vagus resected (SV, n = 11), RYGB (n = 12), and RYGB without preservation of the vagus (RYGBV, n = 12). Body mass, fasting blood glucose (FBG), fasting serum insulin, and concentrations of fasting serum GLP-1 were examined in the first, second, fourth, and eighth week before and after surgery. The effects of RYGB and the hepatic branch of the vagus on GLP-1 levels in the eighth postoperative week were also analyzed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "RYGB caused a significant reduction in the weight of rats with T2DM (P < 0.05), improved the levels of serum GLP-1 and insulin (P < 0.05), and decreased FBG level (P < 0.05). Retention of the hepatic branch of the vagus maintained weight reduction for a longer period (P < 0.05) and increased the levels of serum GLP-1 and insulin (P < 0.05), but had no impact on FBG level (P > 0.05)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "RYGB had better therapeutic efficacy in rats with T2DM. Care should be taken during RYGB surgery to preserve the hepatic branch of the vagus."
          }
        ]
      },
